 { In this first section, the focus is going to be more on understanding the significance of weak squares. The examples will focus on three aspects of weak squares: 1. Weak squares and attacking 2. Weak squares and knights 3. Weak squares and outposts As chess players, we all have an intuitive grasp of the significance of weak squares. We have all have won and lost many games due to the weakness of one or several important squares in the past, even if we may not have been thinking about it in these terms at the time. Take the following example for instance. The White player might describe their compensation for being two pawns down as 'having attacking possibilities'. } 1. { -KEY- } Bd5 $1 { Indeed, it's no surprise that here White is equal and even has the better practical chances after this aggressive move. If this were an attacking manual, we might speak about the target on f7 as a 'focal point' for White's attack. However, at the heart of these offensive possibilities is the presence of multiple weak squares on the Black kingside. If Black had a different pawn structure, White's bishop might not be able to occupy d5 while White's queen might not have access to f6. However, since these are both weak squares, White is able to secure great placements for their pieces and use this to drum up a powerful attack. This link between a positional feature like the structure, and a dynamic factor like piece activity, brings us to an important point that will be discussed throughout the course. Namely, that there is a deep link between positional play and dynamic attacking play, and you cannot truly separate the two. After all, a positional asset like a weak square is valuable not for its own sake but because it can be used as a springboard for tactical and attacking operations either immediately or in the future. Let's continue with the position for a few more moves. } Qg7 { Seeking a queen exchange to defuse the attack. } 2. Bxf7+ Kh8 3. Qg5 $1 { The White queen shuffles to the g5 square, the next closest available weak square. From here, there is only one move that saves Black. } Rf8 $2 { In the game, Black went wrong and targeted the bishop. However, now the presence of all the weak squares in the Black camp leads to an easy path to victory for White. } ( 3... Rb7 $1 { The only way to stay in the game! Black spots a clever tactic based around the poor placement of White's king on h1. } 4. Rxb7 ( 3... Rf8 3. Re8 Kh7 5. Bg8 Qxg8 ( { Black has to give up the queen, since } 5... Kh8 { loses to } 6. Bd5 { . } ) 
6. Rxg8 Kxg8 $11 { Black's rook, bishop and pawn offer full compensation for the queen. } ) 
Be4 $11 5. Kh2 Bxb7 6. Bxg6 Kg8 7. Qxh5 Qh8 $11 { With an equal endgame, in spite of being a pawn down. } ) 
4. Bd5 $1 Qa1+ { Black escapes the attack with check, but now there is no way to stop mate. } 5. Kh2 $18 { Black has no good reply to the threat of mate on h6. In this example, White was able to slice through the Black position due to the weakness of not just one, but a set of squares. In particular, the dark squares on f6, g5 and h6 were all weak. This topic is explored in detail in the section on 'Color Complexes' towards the end of the chapter. } 
