 { At first sight, it might appear that White is in trouble here as their knight on e4 is pinned. However, it turns out that Black has a weak square that can be exploited. Can you find the best move here for White? } 1. { -KEY- } Bg5 $1 { The only move, but a very powerful one. White exploits the weak square on f6. } Qd7 { Black steps away from the attack and tries to blockade White's passed pawn on d6. } ( 1... f6 $2 { The f6 square cannot be patched up by a pawn because of the following tactic: } 2. Nxf6 $1 Bxf6 3. Rxe8 $1 { Now the queen is distracted from the defense of the f6 bishop. } Qxe8 4. Bxf6 $18 { White is two pawns to the good with a completely winning position. } ) 
2. Nf6+ $1 { Using the weak square to force Black to part ways with their powerful dark squared bishop. } Bxf6 3. Bxf6 { Now there are numerous weakened dark squares around the Black king. } Nc8 { Targeting the d6 pawn. } 4. Rxe8+ Qxe8 5. Qc5 $1 { Complete board vision! White spots that the c5 square is also weak, and uses it to bring the queen into the game. } Qe6 6. Bc3 $1 { Preparing the queen-bishop battery. } ( 6. Be7 $2 { would throw away all of White's advantage after } Nxe7 7. dxe7 Re8 $11 { when Black will regain the pawn while maintaining reasonable piece coordination. } ) 
Qxd6 7. Qd4 $1 { Now White takes advantage of the weak square on d4! } f6 { Forced, to avoid being checkmated. } 8. Qxf6 Qxf6 9. Bxf6 $18 { Black survives the attack along the dark squares, but the resulting endgame is lost. The problem for Black in the starting position was their very poor dark-squared control, a weakness which White's pieces were well-placed to take advantage of. } 
