 { One of the reasons why weak squares can be very valuable is that they can provide a stable post for an enemy piece. Of all the pieces, knights in particular tend to benefit the most. This is because the knight is a short range piece that cannot defend itself against the other pieces on the board due to its unique L-shaped movements. Landing a knight on a weak square can be a huge achievement and shape the entire course of a game. This can happen even in cases where the knight is on a less advanced post. Let's take a look at a couple of examples of this in action. } 7. { -KEY- } Nc4 $1 { The White knight lands on the weak square, where it is very stable and has a major influence on the game. It protects the a5 pawn, controls the d6 square, and defends the base of the White pawn chain on b2. With the knight as the glue keeping the position safe, White will soon use their rooks to target the weak pawn on a6. } e4 { Black tries to open lines of attack in order to dislodge the knight. } 8. Rda8 { Simplest. White goes after the stranded pawn. } ( 8. fxe4 $6 { would make things more messy after } Rxe4 { . } ) 
exf3 9. Kxf3 Nd6 { Black needs to fight against the knight, but with this move they invite a mating attack. } 10. Rg8+ $1 Kh5 ( 10... Kf5 11. g4 ) 
11. g4+ Kh4 12. Rg6 $18 { There is no defense against mate along the h-file. } 
