 { This position is taken from one of my games in the 2018 Irish Championship, where I had the White pieces against IM Sam Collins. In spite of Black having the bishop pair and control of the open d-file, the position is roughly balanced due to White's superior structure. } 24. { -KEY- } Bb5 $1 { Robbing Black of his most active bishop and, at the same time, freeing up the weak c4 square for a new purpose in the future. } Bxb5 25. Qxb5 Rb8 26. Qa4 Qd8 { Black is trying to generate play along the b- and d-files. } 27. Ne3 $1 { The knight is ready to land on the weak c4 square next. } Rdb7 ( 27... Rd2 $2 { Black would be pushed back immediately if they enter, since after } 28. Nc4 $16 { Black will have to retreat his rook. } ) 
28. Rd1 { First taking control of the open file. } Qc7 29. Nc4 $16 { White completes his plan, and as a result Black's counterplay along the b- and d-files has dried up. White will soon recover their missing pawn and have a clear advantage owing to the superior minor piece and healthier structure. Notice how, as in the previous example, the knight plays an important role in both defense (the b2 pawn) and attack (a5 pawn, d6 & e5 squares). } 
