 { The third topic that we'll discuss within this section is the link between weak squares & outposts, and for this we'll continue with the theme of a knight on the c4 square. Outposts are the topic of the next chapter, and are defined as 'a square on the fourth, fifth, sixth, or seventh rank which is protected by a pawn and which cannot be attacked by an opponent's pawn'. In short, an outpost is a weak square that happens to also be protected by a pawn. This position, near identical to the one examined recently, occurred in the game Anand,V - Ivanchuk,V (2001) and is the one which I modified earlier. We can see that the evaluation here is similar to the previous example. When weighing up whether to keep a square as a weak square or to convert it into an outpost, there are a few things you can consider: 1. An outpost is usually more valuable as it offers greater stability and control, so if there are no downsides, it will generally be a good idea to create an outpost. 2. Downsides however are common. Pushing pawns can give your opponent a weak square or outpost to operate on. In this example, we can see Black now has the c3 outpost. However, this is not a concern for White here since Black cannot access it. 3. If a piece is on a weak square that cannot be targeted, it is effectively as good as an outpost. In such cases, it is often not necessary to create an outpost. Let's now take a look at how Vishy Anand converted this position. } 2. { -KEY- } Rda8 { Wasting no time in targeting the weak pawn. } h4 3. Rxa6 Nc7 4. Rxe6 Nxe6 5. a6 { The passed a-pawn will cost Black his knight. } Nc7 6. Ra7 $18 { Black resigned, since a move like ...Nd5 will be met by Rd7 followed by a6-a7. We can see that this game followed a similarly brutal trajectory as our modified example from earlier, and that it did not matter whether the c4 square was a weak square or a full-fledged outpost. There is one more common decision point that you will face when considering weak squares vs outposts, which we'll now turn to discuss in our final example of the section. } 
 *

