[Result "*"]
[FEN "r1bqr1k1/1p1n1pbp/2p2np1/3pp3/p3P3/P1PP1NP1/1PQ2PBP/R1B1RNK1 b - - 0 1"]

 { In your games, you may often have a choice between targeting an outpost or targeting a weak square. In the following example, Black could play ...Nc5-b3 to reach the outpost. However, they have an even stronger plan based on weak squares. } 1... { -KEY- } dxe4 $1 { By exchanging central pawns, Black forcibly creates a weak square on d3. } ( 1... Nc5 { Targeting the outpost is not unreasonable, but it doesn't pack as much of a punch. } 2. Be3 Nb3 3. Rad1 $13 { Black has reached the outpost, but White has a solid piece configuration and can challenge the knight on the next move with Nf1-d2. It's important to try to figure out what our opponent's defensive plan might be when targeting a specific square. } ) 
2. dxe4 Nc5 $1 { Black now has the luxury of choice as they can move to either the weak square on d3 next, or the outpost on b3. } 3. Bg5 { White tries to complete their development. } ( 3. Rd1 $6 { This move would cover the d3 square, but leave the e4 pawn undefended. } Qe7 $17 { It proves very awkward to defend the e4 pawn. For instance, moves like Nf3-d2 or Nf1-d2 hem in White's dark-squared bishop, while an indirect defense with the move Bc1-g5 will force White to concede the bishop pair after ...h6. } ) 
Qd3 $1 { This method for targeting the enemy squares on d3/d6 by the Black queen is a frequently recurring theme. } ( 3... Nd3 $4 4. Red1 { The pin costs Black a full piece. } ) 
4. Qxd3 { Black has too much pressure against e4, so the queen exchange is forced. } Nxd3 $17 { The weak d3 square proves to be a much more effective target than the b3 outpost. From here the knight is more centrally placed and arrives on the square with a double attack against the White pieces. White will have to move the rook to e2 to hold things together, but soon Black's bishops will come to the party (...Be6-c4, ...Bf8-c5) which will strain the White position further. Black is close to winning. Knowing which squares to choose comes with practice, and with familiarity over a range of different positions. However, there are some useful rules of thumb or questions that you may wish to ask yourself to help to guide you. Below is a partial list: 1. Between the weak square and the outpost, which is more advanced? 2. Which is more central? 3. Does occupying either square come with a threat against the enemy's position? 4. How does occupation of each square link up with our broader strategy? 5. How easily can our opponent challenge or dislodge our piece? In the case of the example we have just covered, the d3 square was more attractive, as from here Black entered the position with several threats while White's pieces were not well-placed to challenge the knight. This concludes our first section of this chapter. In the next section, we'll be focusing on identifying and targeting weak squares. See you there! } 
